{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Grace, the pertinent information is: adaptive: support with time, money, and planning; sensory: no primary concern; medical: none affecting school; and social: vulnerable to being overly trusting. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: abstract vocabulary; remembering multi-step procedures; reading and mathematics at Grade 6 complexity; and transferring skills to new contexts. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Grace, the most useful EA actions are to check genuine understanding; teach self-advocacy and safe decision-making; use age-respectful materials; and fade prompts and celebrate independence. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-K-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 26}, {"text": "in